":26.63308048248291,"seed":769270697646158976,"stop":"eos","text":"Next, we use the known values of the sines:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\sin 15^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} = \\sin 75^\\circ = \\sin (90^\\circ - 15^\\circ) = \\cos 15^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute these values into the expression:\n\n\\[\nS = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nS = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2} + 2\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nS = 2 \\left( \\frac{2\\sqrt{6} + 2\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nS = 2 \\left( \\frac{2(\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2})}{4} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nS = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nS = \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the given problem, we need to compute the sum of the sines of the angles \\(\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\), \\(\\frac{3\\pi}{12}\\), \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\), \\(\\frac{7\\pi}{12}\\), \\(\\frac{9\\pi}{12}\\), and \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{12}\\). Let's denote this sum by \\(S\\):\n\n\\[\nS = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we use the identity \\(\\sin(\\pi - x) = \\sin x\\). This allows us to pair the terms in the sum as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{3\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\nSo the sum \\(S\\) can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\nS = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\right) + \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{3\\pi}{12}\\right) + \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nS = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\\[\nS = 2 \\left( \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} \\right)\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the known values of the sines:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\sin 15^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} = \\sin 75^\\circ = \\sin (90^\\circ - 15^\\circ) = \\cos 15^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute these values into the expression:\n\n\\[\nS = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nS = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2} + 2\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nS = 2 \\left( \\frac{2\\sqrt{6} + 2\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nS = 2 \\left( \\frac{2(\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2})}{4} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nS = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\\[\nS = \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}\n\\]","id":"precalculus:416"}
